{\n88:     insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"enum E { safe(Vec<E>), bad(E) }\\n\\n0\"));\n89: }\n\n(Showing lines 60-89 of 200. Use offset=90 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"fn an_enum_whose_payload_mentions_itself() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"enum E { leaf, node{next: E} }\\n\\nleaf\"));\n}\n\n/// `Json`'s array case (kantord/toylang#76, `plans/mini-parser-spike.md`'s open question):\n/// behind a `Vec`, a self-reference is a heap indirection rather than an infinite layout, so\n/// this rules the other way from the bare-field case above. Construction and matching both\n/// have to type-check, not just the declaration -- `describe`'s nested `Json.num(1)` is exactly\n/// where a stub standing in for the recursive occurrence's own variant list would otherwise\n/// come apart. Running the value that stub describes is the backends' half, settled in\n/// kantord/toylang#94 and pinned by the corpus's `enum_recursive_*` cases.\n#[test]\nfn a_vec_wrapped_self_reference_typechecks() {\n    assert!(\n        toylang::compile(\n            \"enum Json { arr(Vec<Json>), num(Int) }\\n\\n\\\n             fn describe(j: Json) -> Str = j | arr -> \\\"arr\\\" or num -> \\\"num\\\"\\n\\n\\\n             describe(Json.arr([Json.num(1)]))\"\n        )\n        .is_ok()","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/tests/enums.rs","text":"fn an_enum_whose_payload_mentions_itself() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"enum E { leaf, node{next: E} }\\n\\nleaf\"));\n}\n\n/// `Json`'s array case (kantord/toylang#76, `plans/mini-parser-spike.md`'s open question):\n/// behind a `Vec`, a self-reference is a heap indirection rather than an infinite layout, so\n/// this rules the other way from the bare-field case above. Construction and matching both\n/// have to type-check, not just the declaration -- `describe`'s nested `Json.num(1)` is exactly\n/// where a stub standing in for the recursive occurrence's own variant list would otherwise\n/// come apart. Running the value that stub describes is the backends' half, settled in\n/// kantord/toylang#94 and pinned by the corpus's `enum_recursive_*` cases.\n#[test]\nfn a_vec_wrapped_self_reference_typechecks() {\n    assert!(\n        toylang::compile(\n            \"enum Json { arr(Vec<Json>), num(Int) }\\n\\n\\\n             fn describe(j: Json) -> Str = j | arr -> \\\"arr\\\" or num -> \\\"num\\\"\\n\\n\\\n             describe(Json.arr([Json.num(1)]))\"\n        )\n        .is_ok()\n    );\n}\n\n/// The same refusal from `an_enum_whose_payload_mentions_itself`, still standing on a specific\n/// variant even when a different variant of the same enum is legally `Vec`-boxed: whether a\n/// self-reference is behind a `Vec` is judged per occurrence, not per declaration.\n#[test]\nfn only_the_boxed_variant_of_a_mixed_enum_is_legal() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(err(\"enum E { safe(Vec<E>), bad(E) }\\n\\n0\"));\n}","lineStart":60,"lineEnd":89,"totalLines":200,"truncated":true}},"title":"tests/enums.rs","time":{"start":1788213616271,"end":1788213616282}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Interesting. There's a real failure: `a_vec_wrapped_self_reference_typechecks` in tests/enums.rs:73. This is NOT a .snap offset issue - it's an actual compile test. The test program:\n\n```\nenum Json { arr(Vec<Json>), num(Int) }\n\nfn describe(j: Json) -> Str = j | arr -> \"arr\" or num -> \"num\"\n\ndescribe(Json.arr([Json.num(1)]))\n```\n\nThis is a runtime/parse test - `toylang::compile(...).is_ok()`. This is an inline string in a test that needs a semicolon terminator now. The prior runs may have missed this because it's an inline test string, not a corpus file.\n\nLet me look at the test file.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_059d65b60001Io3iayyCudyl16","sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","messageID":"msg_059d64cf8001IOi2GMDL0YL7k9"}}
