{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788213674527,"sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8e54230d32c17a3b","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cat docs/examples/euler/01-multiples-of-3-and-5.md"},"output":"# Multiples of 3 and 5 below 1000\n\nSolves [Project Euler 1](https://projecteuler.net/problem=1). See the\n[spoiler warning](00-spoiler-warning.md).\n\nNo fold or loop needed: the sum of the multiples of `k` below `limit` is a triangular number,\nscaled, and inclusion-exclusion handles the double-counted multiples of 15. The obvious\nfilter -- `select(. % 3 == 0 or . % 5 == 0)` over the whole range -- works too, but the\nclosed form never scans at all.\n\n```toylang\nfn triangle(m: Int) -> Int = m * (m + 1) / 2\n\nfn sum_of_multiples(p: {k: Int, limit: Int}) -> Int =\n    triangle((p.limit - 1) / p.k) * p.k\n\nsum_of_multiples({k: 3, limit: 1000}) + sum_of_multiples({k: 5, limit: 1000}) -\n    sum_of_multiples({k: 15, limit: 1000})\n```\n\n```output\n233168\n```\n","metadata":{"output":"# Multiples of 3 and 5 below 1000\n\nSolves [Project Euler 1](https://projecteuler.net/problem=1). See the\n[spoiler warning](00-spoiler-warning.md).\n\nNo fold or loop needed: the sum of the multiples of `k` below `limit` is a triangular number,\nscaled, and inclusion-exclusion handles the double-counted multiples of 15. The obvious\nfilter -- `select(. % 3 == 0 or . % 5 == 0)` over the whole range -- works too, but the\nclosed form never scans at all.\n\n```toylang\nfn triangle(m: Int) -> Int = m * (m + 1) / 2\n\nfn sum_of_multiples(p: {k: Int, limit: Int}) -> Int =\n    triangle((p.limit - 1) / p.k) * p.k\n\nsum_of_multiples({k: 3, limit: 1000}) + sum_of_multiples({k: 5, limit: 1000}) -\n    sum_of_multiples({k: 15, limit: 1000})\n```\n\n```output\n233168\n```\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cat docs/examples/euler/01-multiples-of-3-and-5.md","time":{"start":1788213674523,"end":1788213674525}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The maintainer sample has two fn definitions, each missing `;`. The error is at span 46-48 \"expected `;`, found `fn`\" — that's the boundary between the first fn's body `... / 2` and the second `fn`. So both fn bodies need `;`.\n\nThis is a docs example (`docs/examples/euler/01-multiples-of-3-and-5.md`). The issue says update docs/reference/syntax. But also this test pins the sample verbatim. If I change this test, I should also update the corresponding docs example to keep them consistent. But wait — is the docs example checked by fmt_examples test (`every_docs_fragment_is_already_formatted` passed)? It passed, meaning the docs fragment... hmm, but this sample in the test is `docs/examples/euler/01-multiples-of-3-and-5.md`. If that docs file has fn without `;`, then `every_docs_fragment_is_already_formatted` should fail too. But it passed. \n\nWait, maybe the docs file was already updated by a prior run (or it's different). Let me check the docs file. Actually the fmt_examples test passed which means every docs fragment is formatted. But the sample string here in the test doesn't have `;`. These are two different things — the test pins the string verbatim AND asserts fmt(sample)==sample. If the sample is now invalid (no `;`), fmt fails.\n\nBut wait, if the docs file has `;` and the sample string here doesn't, they'd be inconsistent. Let me check the docs file.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_059d740a0001OjDvjm5xmlYVUk","sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","messageID":"msg_059d72fe90011JtBc2lzV62n2l"}}
