{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788213687221,"sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-af0b87fd5800aa7f","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/src/emit_toylang.rs","limit":75,"offset":161},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/src/emit_toylang.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n161: pub fn emit(file: &File) -> String {\n162:     let mut out = String::new();\n163:     for decl in decls_in_source_order(&file.aliases, &file.enums, &file.defs) {\n164:         out.push_str(&decl);\n165:         out.push_str(\"\\n\\n\");\n166:     }\n167:     out.push_str(&print_expr_wrapped(&file.body, Ctx::Expr(0), 0));\n168:     out.push('\\n');\n169:     out\n170: }\n171: \n172: /// A module is the declarations alone: no trailing expression to separate them from, so they end\n173: /// the file rather than each being followed by a blank line the way `emit` writes them.\n174: pub fn emit_module(module: &Module) -> String {\n175:     let decls = decls_in_source_order(&[], &module.enums, &module.defs);\n176:     if decls.is_empty() {\n177:         return String::new();\n178:     }\n179:     format!(\"{}\\n\", decls.join(\"\\n\\n\"))\n180: }\n181: \n182: /// Every declaration rendered, back in the order it was written.\n183: ///\n184: /// The AST groups declarations by kind, losing their interleaving in the source; sorting by span\n185: /// start puts them back, which is what makes the output idempotent -- reformatting an\n186: /// already-sorted file is a no-op re-sort.\n187: fn decls_in_source_order(aliases: &[Alias], enums: &[EnumDecl], defs: &[Def]) -> Vec<String> {\n188:     enum Item<'a> {\n189:         Alias(&'a Alias),\n190:         Enum(&'a EnumDecl),\n191:         Def(&'a Def),\n192:     }\n193: \n194:     let mut items: Vec<(usize, Item)> = Vec::new();\n195:     for a in aliases {\n196:         items.push((a.span.start, Item::Alias(a)));\n197:     }\n198:     for e in enums {\n199:         items.push((e.span.start, Item::Enum(e)));\n200:     }\n201:     for d in defs {\n202:         items.push((d.span.start, Item::Def(d)));\n203:     }\n204:     items.sort_by_key(|(start, _)| *start);\n205: \n206:     items\n207:         .iter()\n208:         .map(|(_, item)| match item {\n209:             Item::Alias(a) => print_alias(a),\n210:             Item::Enum(e) => print_enum(e),\n211:             Item::Def(d) => print_def(d),\n212:         })\n213:         .collect()\n214: }\n215: \n216: /// Parses `src` and formats it, then reattaches the one piece of source text `emit` cannot\n217: /// reconstruct from the tree: a leading run of `#` comment (and blank) lines, copied back\n218: /// verbatim ahead of the formatted body.\n219: ///\n220: /// Two file shapes reach a formatter that walks a project: a program, and a module --\n221: /// declarations with no trailing expression, which `parse` rejects outright and which\n222: /// `prelude.toy` is the one instance of here. Which one a file is only shows up in the parsing,\n223: /// so both are tried. When neither parse succeeds, the error reported is whichever got further\n224: /// into the file, so a typo halfway down a module is not reported as a missing program body.\n225: pub fn format_source(src: &str) -> Result<String, crate::error::Error> {\n226:     let banner = leading_comment(src);\n227:     let as_program = match crate::parse::parse(src) {\n228:         Ok(file) => return Ok(format!(\"{banner}{}\", emit(&file))),\n229:         Err(e) => e,\n230:     };\n231:     match crate::parse::parse_module(src) {\n232:         Ok(module) => Ok(format!(\"{banner}{}\", emit_module(&module))),\n233:         Err(as_module) if as_module.span.start > as_program.span.start => Err(as_module),\n234:         Err(_) => Err(as_program),\n235:     }\n\n(Showing lines 161-235 of 742. Use offset=236 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"pub fn emit(file: &File) -> String {\n    let mut out = String::new();\n    for decl in decls_in_source_order(&file.aliases, &file.enums, &file.defs) {\n        out.push_str(&decl);\n        out.push_str(\"\\n\\n\");\n    }\n    out.push_str(&print_expr_wrapped(&file.body, Ctx::Expr(0), 0));\n    out.push('\\n');\n    out\n}\n\n/// A module is the declarations alone: no trailing expression to separate them from, so they end\n/// the file rather than each being followed by a blank line the way `emit` writes them.\npub fn emit_module(module: &Module) -> String {\n    let decls = decls_in_source_order(&[], &module.enums, &module.defs);\n    if decls.is_empty() {\n        return String::new();\n    }\n    format!(\"{}\\n\", decls.join(\"\\n\\n\"))\n}","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/src/emit_toylang.rs","text":"pub fn emit(file: &File) -> String {\n    let mut out = String::new();\n    for decl in decls_in_source_order(&file.aliases, &file.enums, &file.defs) {\n        out.push_str(&decl);\n        out.push_str(\"\\n\\n\");\n    }\n    out.push_str(&print_expr_wrapped(&file.body, Ctx::Expr(0), 0));\n    out.push('\\n');\n    out\n}\n\n/// A module is the declarations alone: no trailing expression to separate them from, so they end\n/// the file rather than each being followed by a blank line the way `emit` writes them.\npub fn emit_module(module: &Module) -> String {\n    let decls = decls_in_source_order(&[], &module.enums, &module.defs);\n    if decls.is_empty() {\n        return String::new();\n    }\n    format!(\"{}\\n\", decls.join(\"\\n\\n\"))\n}\n\n/// Every declaration rendered, back in the order it was written.\n///\n/// The AST groups declarations by kind, losing their interleaving in the source; sorting by span\n/// start puts them back, which is what makes the output idempotent -- reformatting an\n/// already-sorted file is a no-op re-sort.\nfn decls_in_source_order(aliases: &[Alias], enums: &[EnumDecl], defs: &[Def]) -> Vec<String> {\n    enum Item<'a> {\n        Alias(&'a Alias),\n        Enum(&'a EnumDecl),\n        Def(&'a Def),\n    }\n\n    let mut items: Vec<(usize, Item)> = Vec::new();\n    for a in aliases {\n        items.push((a.span.start, Item::Alias(a)));\n    }\n    for e in enums {\n        items.push((e.span.start, Item::Enum(e)));\n    }\n    for d in defs {\n        items.push((d.span.start, Item::Def(d)));\n    }\n    items.sort_by_key(|(start, _)| *start);\n\n    items\n        .iter()\n        .map(|(_, item)| match item {\n            Item::Alias(a) => print_alias(a),\n            Item::Enum(e) => print_enum(e),\n            Item::Def(d) => print_def(d),\n        })\n        .collect()\n}\n\n/// Parses `src` and formats it, then reattaches the one piece of source text `emit` cannot\n/// reconstruct from the tree: a leading run of `#` comment (and blank) lines, copied back\n/// verbatim ahead of the formatted body.\n///\n/// Two file shapes reach a formatter that walks a project: a program, and a module --\n/// declarations with no trailing expression, which `parse` rejects outright and which\n/// `prelude.toy` is the one instance of here. Which one a file is only shows up in the parsing,\n/// so both are tried. When neither parse succeeds, the error reported is whichever got further\n/// into the file, so a typo halfway down a module is not reported as a missing program body.\npub fn format_source(src: &str) -> Result<String, crate::error::Error> {\n    let banner = leading_comment(src);\n    let as_program = match crate::parse::parse(src) {\n        Ok(file) => return Ok(format!(\"{banner}{}\", emit(&file))),\n        Err(e) => e,\n    };\n    match crate::parse::parse_module(src) {\n        Ok(module) => Ok(format!(\"{banner}{}\", emit_module(&module))),\n        Err(as_module) if as_module.span.start > as_program.span.start => Err(as_module),\n        Err(_) => Err(as_program),\n    }","lineStart":161,"lineEnd":235,"totalLines":742,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/emit_toylang.rs","time":{"start":1788213687210,"end":1788213687220}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"So the terminator `;` is emitted right after the body: `fn triangle(m: Int) -> Int = m * (m + 1) / 2;`. For the wrapped case, `{sig} =\\n    {body};`.\n\nNow I need to update the maintainer sample. The canonical formatted form would be:\n\n```\nfn triangle(m: Int) -> Int = m * (m + 1) / 2;\nfn sum_of_multiples(p: {k: Int, limit: Int}) -> Int =\n    triangle((p.limit - 1) / p.k) * p.k;\nsum_of_multiples(...) + ... - \n    sum_of_multiples(...)\n```\n\nWait, but does emit add a blank line between declarations? Let me check the emit loop in print... Actually the maintainer sample has blank lines between declarations. Let me check `emit`/`format_source` to see how declarations are joined (with blank lines?). Let me look at lines 161-235.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_059d7724f001DLhr33n1ikzguz","sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","messageID":"msg_059d76872001nhaheTCnItgAUN"}}
