{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788213731974,"sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a6c62748d16e21b2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -n \"reference|fence|run|toylang\" tests/docs.rs | head -40"},"output":"3://! A fragment is a `toylang` fence, an `input` fence if the program reads stdin,\n4://! and exactly one of an `output`, `refuses`, or `error` fence. The first two go through the\n6://! defined in prose; an `error` fence pins the checker's message for a program the docs show\n7://! being turned away. A page can also embed an existing corpus case by id with a `case` fence\n11://! run, not proofread, and drift between the docs and the compiler fails `just test`.\n13://! There is no way for a fragment to opt out of that. A `fixture` fence used to name a\n16://! here had run. A gate that skips silently reads exactly like a check that passed. A claim this\n17://! harness cannot run belongs in an issue, not in an `output` fence\n18://! ([kantord/toylang#129](https://github.com/kantord/toylang/issues/129)).\n28:    /// Compiles, runs on every backend, and they all print this.\n30:    /// Compiles, and every backend refuses to run it. The fence is empty: what each backend\n38:    /// `page:line` of the opening fence, so a failure points at the prose to fix.\n45:/// Fills in a pending fragment's stdin, from an `input` fence: refuses a second one, and a\n46:/// fence with no fragment open to receive it.\n55:        None => panic!(\"{at}: `input` fence with no `toylang` fence before it\"),\n84:                if let Err(e) = toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n95:            Outcome::Error(want) => match toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n127:/// The reference is complete for everything implemented, checkably: a builtin without a page\n130:fn every_builtin_has_a_reference_page() {\n131:    let missing: Vec<&str> = toylang::check::BUILTIN_NAMES\n135:                .join(\"reference/builtins\")\n142:        \"builtins without a reference page under docs/reference/builtins/: {}\",\n191:/// case: a fence that asks for nothing looks exactly like a fence that passes.\n198:    // A `toylang` fence whose expectation fence has not arrived yet.\n215:                None => panic!(\"{at}: fence never closes\"),\n219:            \"toylang\" => {\n221:                    panic!(\"{prev}: fragment has no `output`, `refuses`, or `error` fence\");\n229:                        \"{at}: `{}` fence with no `toylang` fence before it\",\n239:                            \"{at}: a `refuses` fence is empty; what each backend says while \\\n256:                    \"{at}: a `case` fence holds one corpus case id\"\n265:        panic!(\"{prev}: fragment has no `output`, `refuses`, or `error` fence\");\n","metadata":{"output":"3://! A fragment is a `toylang` fence, an `input` fence if the program reads stdin,\n4://! and exactly one of an `output`, `refuses`, or `error` fence. The first two go through the\n6://! defined in prose; an `error` fence pins the checker's message for a program the docs show\n7://! being turned away. A page can also embed an existing corpus case by id with a `case` fence\n11://! run, not proofread, and drift between the docs and the compiler fails `just test`.\n13://! There is no way for a fragment to opt out of that. A `fixture` fence used to name a\n16://! here had run. A gate that skips silently reads exactly like a check that passed. A claim this\n17://! harness cannot run belongs in an issue, not in an `output` fence\n18://! ([kantord/toylang#129](https://github.com/kantord/toylang/issues/129)).\n28:    /// Compiles, runs on every backend, and they all print this.\n30:    /// Compiles, and every backend refuses to run it. The fence is empty: what each backend\n38:    /// `page:line` of the opening fence, so a failure points at the prose to fix.\n45:/// Fills in a pending fragment's stdin, from an `input` fence: refuses a second one, and a\n46:/// fence with no fragment open to receive it.\n55:        None => panic!(\"{at}: `input` fence with no `toylang` fence before it\"),\n84:                if let Err(e) = toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n95:            Outcome::Error(want) => match toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n127:/// The reference is complete for everything implemented, checkably: a builtin without a page\n130:fn every_builtin_has_a_reference_page() {\n131:    let missing: Vec<&str> = toylang::check::BUILTIN_NAMES\n135:                .join(\"reference/builtins\")\n142:        \"builtins without a reference page under docs/reference/builtins/: {}\",\n191:/// case: a fence that asks for nothing looks exactly like a fence that passes.\n198:    // A `toylang` fence whose expectation fence has not arrived yet.\n215:                None => panic!(\"{at}: fence never closes\"),\n219:            \"toylang\" => {\n221:                    panic!(\"{prev}: fragment has no `output`, `refuses`, or `error` fence\");\n229:                        \"{at}: `{}` fence with no `toylang` fence before it\",\n239:                            \"{at}: a `refuses` fence is empty; what each backend says while \\\n256:                    \"{at}: a `case` fence holds one corpus case id\"\n265:        panic!(\"{prev}: fragment has no `output`, `refuses`, or `error` fence\");\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"rg -n \"reference|fence|run|toylang\" tests/docs.rs | head -40","time":{"start":1788213731968,"end":1788213731971}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_059d82181001Co2g0W7zI7BEBI","sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","messageID":"msg_059d81def001AxLDX5dU0JlpBC"}}
