{"type":"text","timestamp":1788213738140,"sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","part":{"id":"prt_059d83979001Lt6axtT7zgtMED","messageID":"msg_059d822ad0010K8uNytICF2F28","sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","type":"text","text":"The docs harness runs every fragment, so all `fn`/`type` declaration examples must now include the terminator or the harness will fail. Let me update the reference/types docs.\n\n","time":{"start":1788213737849,"end":1788213738138}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788213738145,"sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-aefddad861251912","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/types/opt.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/types/opt.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Opt\n2: \n3: `Opt<T>`: a `T` that may be absent. The prelude declares it as an ordinary\n4: [enum](enum.md) -- `enum Opt<T> { some(T), none }` -- so absence is a tag the value carries\n5: in memory, the same as any other enum's variant, not a null pointer standing in for a\n6: missing `T`. Most `Opt`s come from an operation that cannot promise an entry -- a\n7: collapsing index, [`tail`](../builtins/tail.md), a projection through a ragged dimension --\n8: and the constructors `some(x)` and `none` spell one directly.\n9: \n10: ```toylang\n11: str(range(5)[3]!)\n12: ```\n13: \n14: ```output\n15: 3\n16: ```\n17: \n18: `!` is the one consumer: it insists the value is there, yields the `T`, and if the value is\n19: absent every backend refuses at runtime (see [unwrap](../operators/unwrap.md)).\n20: \n21: `Opt` is in the type grammar, so a function may declare one as a parameter or return type,\n22: which is what lets it hand the absence back instead of being forced to insist:\n23: \n24: ```toylang\n25: fn head(v: Vec<Int>) -> Opt<Int> = v[0]\n26: \n27: str(head([1, 2, 3])!)\n28: ```\n29: \n30: ```output\n31: 1\n32: ```\n33: \n34: An unconsumed `Opt` can be the program's result, and absence prints as `null`:\n35: \n36: ```toylang\n37: [1, 2, 3][9]\n38: ```\n39: \n40: ```output\n41: null\n42: ```\n43: \n44: Absence is not emptiness. An empty `Vec` that is present prints `[]`; only a missing entry\n45: prints `null`:\n46: \n47: ```case\n48: opt_holds_an_empty_vec\n49: ```\n50: \n51: `Opt` nests the same way any other type does -- nothing in the grammar singles out one\n52: level -- so `Opt<Opt<T>>` is exactly as legal as `Opt<T>`, and its two levels are two honest\n53: tags, not the same null collapsed twice: `some(none)` and `none` are different values in\n54: memory. `!` peels exactly one level, so it can tell them apart. Unwrapping a `some(none)`\n55: survives, because the outer tag says the value is there, and yields the inner absence:\n56: \n57: ```toylang\n58: fn shallow() -> Opt<Opt<Int>> = some(none)\n59: \n60: shallow()!\n61: ```\n62: \n63: ```output\n64: null\n65: ```\n66: \n67: while unwrapping a bare `none` refuses, because there is no outer value to yield:\n68: \n69: ```toylang\n70: fn shallow() -> Opt<Opt<Int>> = none\n71: \n72: shallow()!\n73: ```\n74: \n75: ```refuses\n76: ```\n77: \n78: Both programs print `null` if the `!` is dropped; the tag in memory is the only thing that\n79: tells them apart, and `!` is what reaches it.\n80: \n81: Serialization does not see any of this. Printing an `Opt` is the one place it is\n82: special-cased against the rest of the enum system: every other enum's payload variant is a\n83: single-key wrapper and its unit variant a bare string\n84: ([ADR 0009](../../adr/0009-enums-are-json-native-single-key-wrappers.md)), but `Opt`'s\n85: `some(x)` prints as `x` itself and `none` prints as `null` -- no wrapper, and no tag\n86: surviving onto the wire. The rule predates the general one: `Opt` used to be the\n87: value-or-`null` representation in full, and became a tagged-in-memory prelude enum only\n88: later, keeping the old wire form as the one exception ADR 0009 records.\n89: \n90: Because printing drops the tag, it drops it at every level a nested `Opt` has. A present\n91: value inside an absent-looking one and a value that is absent all the way down print\n92: identically:\n93: \n94: ```toylang\n95: fn deep() -> Opt<Opt<Int>> = some(some(7))\n96: \n97: fn shallow() -> Opt<Opt<Int>> = some(none)\n98: \n99: fn absent() -> Opt<Opt<Int>> = none\n100: \n101: {a: deep(), b: shallow(), c: absent()}\n102: ```\n103: \n104: ```output\n105: {\"a\":7,\"b\":null,\"c\":null}\n106: ```\n107: \n108: `b` and `c` are different values -- the `!` examples above tell them apart -- but nothing\n109: in `{\"b\":null,\"c\":null}` says so. That is the cost of the wire form:\n110: it is lossy the same way every type-level distinction in the output already is\n111: ([kantord/toylang#62](https://github.com/kantord/toylang/issues/62)), and `Opt` pays it\n112: twice over when it nests.\n113: \n114: Rust's `Option<T>` is the same tagged enum, `enum Option<T> { Some(T), None }`, and the two\n115: languages agree deeper than the name: `serde_json`'s default `Serialize` for `Option<T>`\n116: prints `Some(x)` as `x` and `None` as `null`, so `Option<Option<i32>>` collapses the same\n117: way `Opt<Opt<Int>>` does here -- `Some(None)` and `None` both print `null`. The difference\n118: is where that rule lives. In toylang it is the language's own printer, with no other choice\n119: available. In Rust, `Option<T>` has no wire format of its own; `serde` is a library, its\n120: `null`-for-`None` behavior is one derive among others, and a type that wants a different\n121: shape on the wire picks a different one:\n122: \n123: ```rust\n124: #[derive(serde::Serialize)]\n125: enum Shape {\n126:     Circle { r: i32 },\n127: }\n128: ```\n129: \n130: which prints the same externally-tagged wrapper toylang's own\n131: [enums](enum.md) do, `{\"Circle\":{\"r\":1}}`, because that shape was `serde`'s choice to make,\n132: not `Option`'s.\n\n(End of file - total 132 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Opt\n\n`Opt<T>`: a `T` that may be absent. The prelude declares it as an ordinary\n[enum](enum.md) -- `enum Opt<T> { some(T), none }` -- so absence is a tag the value carries\nin memory, the same as any other enum's variant, not a null pointer standing in for a\nmissing `T`. Most `Opt`s come from an operation that cannot promise an entry -- a\ncollapsing index, [`tail`](../builtins/tail.md), a projection through a ragged dimension --\nand the constructors `some(x)` and `none` spell one directly.\n\n```toylang\nstr(range(5)[3]!)\n```\n\n```output\n3\n```\n\n`!` is the one consumer: it insists the value is there, yields the `T`, and if the value is\nabsent every backend refuses at runtime (see [unwrap](../operators/unwrap.md)).\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/types/opt.md","text":"# Opt\n\n`Opt<T>`: a `T` that may be absent. The prelude declares it as an ordinary\n[enum](enum.md) -- `enum Opt<T> { some(T), none }` -- so absence is a tag the value carries\nin memory, the same as any other enum's variant, not a null pointer standing in for a\nmissing `T`. Most `Opt`s come from an operation that cannot promise an entry -- a\ncollapsing index, [`tail`](../builtins/tail.md), a projection through a ragged dimension --\nand the constructors `some(x)` and `none` spell one directly.\n\n```toylang\nstr(range(5)[3]!)\n```\n\n```output\n3\n```\n\n`!` is the one consumer: it insists the value is there, yields the `T`, and if the value is\nabsent every backend refuses at runtime (see [unwrap](../operators/unwrap.md)).\n\n`Opt` is in the type grammar, so a function may declare one as a parameter or return type,\nwhich is what lets it hand the absence back instead of being forced to insist:\n\n```toylang\nfn head(v: Vec<Int>) -> Opt<Int> = v[0]\n\nstr(head([1, 2, 3])!)\n```\n\n```output\n1\n```\n\nAn unconsumed `Opt` can be the program's result, and absence prints as `null`:\n\n```toylang\n[1, 2, 3][9]\n```\n\n```output\nnull\n```\n\nAbsence is not emptiness. An empty `Vec` that is present prints `[]`; only a missing entry\nprints `null`:\n\n```case\nopt_holds_an_empty_vec\n```\n\n`Opt` nests the same way any other type does -- nothing in the grammar singles out one\nlevel -- so `Opt<Opt<T>>` is exactly as legal as `Opt<T>`, and its two levels are two honest\ntags, not the same null collapsed twice: `some(none)` and `none` are different values in\nmemory. `!` peels exactly one level, so it can tell them apart. Unwrapping a `some(none)`\nsurvives, because the outer tag says the value is there, and yields the inner absence:\n\n```toylang\nfn shallow() -> Opt<Opt<Int>> = some(none)\n\nshallow()!\n```\n\n```output\nnull\n```\n\nwhile unwrapping a bare `none` refuses, because there is no outer value to yield:\n\n```toylang\nfn shallow() -> Opt<Opt<Int>> = none\n\nshallow()!\n```\n\n```refuses\n```\n\nBoth programs print `null` if the `!` is dropped; the tag in memory is the only thing that\ntells them apart, and `!` is what reaches it.\n\nSerialization does not see any of this. Printing an `Opt` is the one place it is\nspecial-cased against the rest of the enum system: every other enum's payload variant is a\nsingle-key wrapper and its unit variant a bare string\n([ADR 0009](../../adr/0009-enums-are-json-native-single-key-wrappers.md)), but `Opt`'s\n`some(x)` prints as `x` itself and `none` prints as `null` -- no wrapper, and no tag\nsurviving onto the wire. The rule predates the general one: `Opt` used to be the\nvalue-or-`null` representation in full, and became a tagged-in-memory prelude enum only\nlater, keeping the old wire form as the one exception ADR 0009 records.\n\nBecause printing drops the tag, it drops it at every level a nested `Opt` has. A present\nvalue inside an absent-looking one and a value that is absent all the way down print\nidentically:\n\n```toylang\nfn deep() -> Opt<Opt<Int>> = some(some(7))\n\nfn shallow() -> Opt<Opt<Int>> = some(none)\n\nfn absent() -> Opt<Opt<Int>> = none\n\n{a: deep(), b: shallow(), c: absent()}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"a\":7,\"b\":null,\"c\":null}\n```\n\n`b` and `c` are different values -- the `!` examples above tell them apart -- but nothing\nin `{\"b\":null,\"c\":null}` says so. That is the cost of the wire form:\nit is lossy the same way every type-level distinction in the output already is\n([kantord/toylang#62](https://github.com/kantord/toylang/issues/62)), and `Opt` pays it\ntwice over when it nests.\n\nRust's `Option<T>` is the same tagged enum, `enum Option<T> { Some(T), None }`, and the two\nlanguages agree deeper than the name: `serde_json`'s default `Serialize` for `Option<T>`\nprints `Some(x)` as `x` and `None` as `null`, so `Option<Option<i32>>` collapses the same\nway `Opt<Opt<Int>>` does here -- `Some(None)` and `None` both print `null`. The difference\nis where that rule lives. In toylang it is the language's own printer, with no other choice\navailable. In Rust, `Option<T>` has no wire format of its own; `serde` is a library, its\n`null`-for-`None` behavior is one derive among others, and a type that wants a different\nshape on the wire picks a different one:\n\n```rust\n#[derive(serde::Serialize)]\nenum Shape {\n    Circle { r: i32 },\n}\n```\n\nwhich prints the same externally-tagged wrapper toylang's own\n[enums](enum.md) do, `{\"Circle\":{\"r\":1}}`, because that shape was `serde`'s choice to make,\nnot `Option`'s.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":132,"totalLines":132,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/reference/types/opt.md","time":{"start":1788213738134,"end":1788213738143}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The docs.rs harness runs every fragment. Each fragment must compile and produce output or refuse with an `error`/`refuses` fence. If a fragment like `fn double(x: Int) -> Int = x * 2` no longer parses (missing `;`), then the `output: 42` fence will fail because compile fails. So these reference/type docs fragments WILL fail `just check` unless they're updated with `;`.\n\nSo I need to update all the `fn`/`type` declaration examples in the docs that are run by this harness. But wait — some of these fragments have an accompanying `output` fence. Those need to compile, so they need `;`.\n\nBut careful: functions.md and programs.md fragments — do they have output fences? functions.md line 14 has `output: 42`. Yes. So those must compile → need `;`. Good, I updated them.\n\nprograms.md has `output: {\"sum\":3}` → updated.\n\nNow the reference/types/*.md fragments. These are run too. Let me update them all. But I should verify each has an output fence or is an `error`/`refuses` example.\n\nLet me handle each file. Let me read them to update the fn declarations. Let me update them one at a time.\n\nActually, let me reconsider scope. The issue says \"Update the corpus-pinned test and docs/reference/syntax accordingly.\" The reference/types docs are part of docs/reference. They have fn declarations that now must have `;`. The docs harness will fail otherwise. So I must update them.\n\nLet me update each file. Let me read them.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_059d83a79001uWl535HRvzKPF4","sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","messageID":"msg_059d822ad0010K8uNytICF2F28"}}
