{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788213758539,"sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-aa54a6dc7f5fe2b5","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/types/int64.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/types/int64.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Int64\n2: \n3: A signed 64-bit integer that wraps on overflow: [Int](int.md)'s rules at twice the width\n4: ([ADR 0010](../../adr/0010-int64-is-a-second-integer-type.md)). It exists for values that are\n5: carried more than computed -- millisecond timestamps, Euler-sized factors -- which `Int`'s\n6: 2.1 billion ceiling turns away.\n7: \n8: Literals carry no suffix. One that fits `Int` is `Int`, and any literal resolves as `Int64`\n9: wherever one is expected -- the same rule that gives `[]` its element type. So the wide\n10: literal below is legal only because the annotation says what it is:\n11: \n12: ```toylang\n13: fn big() -> Int64 = 600851475143\n14: \n15: big()\n16: ```\n17: \n18: ```output\n19: 600851475143\n20: ```\n21: \n22: A wide literal with no such position stays an error; nothing guesses:\n23: \n24: ```toylang\n25: 600851475143\n26: ```\n27: \n28: ```error\n29: integer `600851475143` does not fit in Int, which is 32 bits; only a position that expects Int64 can hold it (at byte 0)\n30: ```\n31: \n32: Nothing widens implicitly. `Int` and `Int64` never meet in one operator, and the error names\n33: [i64](../builtins/i64.md), the one bridge:\n34: \n35: ```toylang\n36: fn big() -> Int64 = 5\n37: \n38: 1 + big()\n39: ```\n40: \n41: ```error\n42: `+` cannot mix Int and Int64; widen the Int side with `i64(...)` (at byte 27)\n43: ```\n44: \n45: A bare literal on either side of an `Int64` operator needs no bridge -- its position already\n46: says which width it has:\n47: \n48: ```toylang\n49: fn big() -> Int64 = 1234567890123456\n50: \n51: big() + 1\n52: ```\n53: \n54: ```output\n55: 1234567890123457\n56: ```\n57: \n58: Arithmetic and comparison work exactly as on `Int`: division truncates toward zero, the\n59: remainder takes the dividend's sign, a zero divisor is the only failure, and everything else\n60: wraps, `MIN / -1` included. A top-level `Int64` result prints as the bare number, and one\n61: inside a record or Vec prints as an ordinary JSON number.\n62: \n63: Two boundaries are part of the contract rather than hidden behind it:\n64: \n65: - `input` cannot be read as `Int64` anywhere in its shape. An `Int64` result prints fine, but\n66:   reading one back is codec design nobody has done -- JavaScript parses JSON numbers into\n67:   doubles and is off past 2^53 -- so the read side is refused until it can be done honestly,\n68:   the same reversible direction [Opt](opt.md) takes.\n69: - The jq backend computes `Int64` arithmetic in IEEE doubles, which are exact only within\n70:   +/-2^53 (about 9.0e15). Inside that envelope jq agrees with the other six backends\n71:   exactly; a program whose values leave it diverges on jq -- in particular the wrap at 2^63\n72:   never happens there, because no double can carry the wrapped value back. The other six\n73:   backends are exact at the full width, and their agreement on the wrapping edges is pinned\n74:   in tests/int64.rs.\n\n(End of file - total 74 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Int64\n\nA signed 64-bit integer that wraps on overflow: [Int](int.md)'s rules at twice the width\n([ADR 0010](../../adr/0010-int64-is-a-second-integer-type.md)). It exists for values that are\ncarried more than computed -- millisecond timestamps, Euler-sized factors -- which `Int`'s\n2.1 billion ceiling turns away.\n\nLiterals carry no suffix. One that fits `Int` is `Int`, and any literal resolves as `Int64`\nwherever one is expected -- the same rule that gives `[]` its element type. So the wide\nliteral below is legal only because the annotation says what it is:\n\n```toylang\nfn big() -> Int64 = 600851475143\n\nbig()\n```\n\n```output\n600851475143\n```","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/types/int64.md","text":"# Int64\n\nA signed 64-bit integer that wraps on overflow: [Int](int.md)'s rules at twice the width\n([ADR 0010](../../adr/0010-int64-is-a-second-integer-type.md)). It exists for values that are\ncarried more than computed -- millisecond timestamps, Euler-sized factors -- which `Int`'s\n2.1 billion ceiling turns away.\n\nLiterals carry no suffix. One that fits `Int` is `Int`, and any literal resolves as `Int64`\nwherever one is expected -- the same rule that gives `[]` its element type. So the wide\nliteral below is legal only because the annotation says what it is:\n\n```toylang\nfn big() -> Int64 = 600851475143\n\nbig()\n```\n\n```output\n600851475143\n```\n\nA wide literal with no such position stays an error; nothing guesses:\n\n```toylang\n600851475143\n```\n\n```error\ninteger `600851475143` does not fit in Int, which is 32 bits; only a position that expects Int64 can hold it (at byte 0)\n```\n\nNothing widens implicitly. `Int` and `Int64` never meet in one operator, and the error names\n[i64](../builtins/i64.md), the one bridge:\n\n```toylang\nfn big() -> Int64 = 5\n\n1 + big()\n```\n\n```error\n`+` cannot mix Int and Int64; widen the Int side with `i64(...)` (at byte 27)\n```\n\nA bare literal on either side of an `Int64` operator needs no bridge -- its position already\nsays which width it has:\n\n```toylang\nfn big() -> Int64 = 1234567890123456\n\nbig() + 1\n```\n\n```output\n1234567890123457\n```\n\nArithmetic and comparison work exactly as on `Int`: division truncates toward zero, the\nremainder takes the dividend's sign, a zero divisor is the only failure, and everything else\nwraps, `MIN / -1` included. A top-level `Int64` result prints as the bare number, and one\ninside a record or Vec prints as an ordinary JSON number.\n\nTwo boundaries are part of the contract rather than hidden behind it:\n\n- `input` cannot be read as `Int64` anywhere in its shape. An `Int64` result prints fine, but\n  reading one back is codec design nobody has done -- JavaScript parses JSON numbers into\n  doubles and is off past 2^53 -- so the read side is refused until it can be done honestly,\n  the same reversible direction [Opt](opt.md) takes.\n- The jq backend computes `Int64` arithmetic in IEEE doubles, which are exact only within\n  +/-2^53 (about 9.0e15). Inside that envelope jq agrees with the other six backends\n  exactly; a program whose values leave it diverges on jq -- in particular the wrap at 2^63\n  never happens there, because no double can carry the wrapped value back. The other six\n  backends are exact at the full width, and their agreement on the wrapping edges is pinned\n  in tests/int64.rs.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":74,"totalLines":74,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/reference/types/int64.md","time":{"start":1788213758522,"end":1788213758536}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now let me update the other files: stream.md, result.md, int64.md, vec.md. Let me read them.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_059d8880d001gGwz1bVgHaE4DD","sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","messageID":"msg_059d8845b00169iQvuVa93hRoZ"}}
