{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788213781606,"sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b46f847b320a9c97","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/types/int64.md","newString":"fn big() -> Int64 = 600851475143;\n\nbig()\n```","oldString":"fn big() -> Int64 = 600851475143\n\nbig()\n```"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/types/int64.md\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/types/int64.md\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/types/int64.md\n@@ -9,9 +9,9 @@\n wherever one is expected -- the same rule that gives `[]` its element type. So the wide\n literal below is legal only because the annotation says what it is:\n \n ```toylang\n-fn big() -> Int64 = 600851475143\n+fn big() -> Int64 = 600851475143;\n \n big()\n ```\n \n","filediff":{"file":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/types/int64.md","patch":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/types/int64.md\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/types/int64.md\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/types/int64.md\n@@ -9,9 +9,9 @@\n wherever one is expected -- the same rule that gives `[]` its element type. So the wide\n literal below is legal only because the annotation says what it is:\n \n ```toylang\n-fn big() -> Int64 = 600851475143\n+fn big() -> Int64 = 600851475143;\n \n big()\n ```\n \n","additions":1,"deletions":1},"truncated":false},"title":"docs/reference/types/int64.md","time":{"start":1788213781598,"end":1788213781602}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"These fragments have error fences with byte offsets too. Let me update the fn declarations:\n- line 13: `fn big() -> Int64 = 600851475143` -> `... 600851475143;`\n- line 36: `fn big() -> Int64 = 5` -> `... 5;`\n- line 49: `fn big() -> Int64 = 1234567890123456` -> `... 1234567890123456;`\n\nAnd the error byte offsets:\n- Line 29: error at byte 0 for `600851475143` (first fragment, no fn before it) - unchanged (byte 0, no fn declaration before).\n- Line 42: error at byte 27 for `1 + big()`. Let me recompute. The program after edit:\n```\nfn big() -> Int64 = 5;\n\n1 + big()\n```\nByte offset of `1` (the `+` mixing error). Let me count: \"fn big() -> Int64 = 5;\" - length. \nf=1,n=2,space=3,b=4,i=5,g=6,(=7,)=8,-=9,>=10,space=11,I=12,n=13,t=14,6=15,4=16,space=17,==18,space=19,5=20,;=21,newline=22,then blank... wait there's a blank line between declarations? No, in the source there's a blank line (line 37 empty). Let me count carefully.\n\nProgram text:\n```\nfn big() -> Int64 = 5;\n<newline>\n<newline>  (blank line)\n1 + big()\n```\n\n\"fn big() -> Int64 = 5;\" = 21 chars (0-20), newline at 21, blank line newline at 22, then \"1\" at byte 23.\n\nWait, is there a blank line? In int64.md, line 36 is `fn big() -> Int64 = 5`, line 37 is empty, line 38 is `1 + big()`. So yes blank line.\n\nSo `1` is at byte 23, not 27. The original was 27 because no `;`. Hmm wait, original:\n```\nfn big() -> Int64 = 5    (20 chars, 0-19)\nnewline 20\nblank newline 21\n1 at 22\n```\nOriginal error said byte 27. That doesn't match either. Let me recount \"fn big() -> Int64 = 5\": f(0)n(1)space(2)b(3)i(4)g(5)((6))(7)space(8)-(9)>(10)space(11)I(12)n(13)t(14)6(15)4(16)space(17)=(18)space(19)5(20) = 21 chars (0-20). newline=21, blank=22, \"1\"=23.\n\nBut the error says byte 27. Hmm, that's confusing. Maybe the error reports the position differently, or maybe there's something I'm miscounting. Actually maybe the error is not about `1` position but about `big()` or the `+` operator. Or maybe the byte offset counts differently.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Rather than hand-compute all byte offsets, let me make the edits and then run `just check` / the docs tests to get the actual compiler-reported offsets, then update the error fences to match.\n\nActually, the cleanest approach: make all fn/type edits across docs, then run the specific docs test to see which error offsets fail, and correct them to the compiler's reported values.\n\nBut wait — the error byte offsets shift only when content BEFORE the error position changes. Adding `;` before the error shifts subsequent offsets by the number of added chars.\n\nFor int64.md line 42: the error is in the second fragment `1 + big()`, and I'm adding `;` to the `fn big() -> Int64 = 5` before it, shifting by +1. So new offset = old 27 + 1 = 28. Let me verify with my count though — my count gave 23. Discrepancy suggests my understanding of the offset is off, OR the original already had the blank-line handling differently.\n\nHmm, let me not overthink. Let me just make all the edits, then run the docs test which will print the actual error, and fix offsets. Let me use the test output to get exact offsets.\n\nActually, simpler: let me just edit the fn lines (adding `;`), then run `cargo test --test docs` to see failures with actual messages, then fix the error fences.\n\nLet me continue editing the other files first.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_059d8e1b800114eQZ3WeKy8mCy","sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","messageID":"msg_059d88a67001CqvuoVylZexuml"}}
