{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788213790359,"sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","part":{"id":"prt_059d9068c0010B1QIoVfev0qwy","messageID":"msg_059d90376001645cGPO2vybcWS","sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","snapshot":"fb191a2e1786af4049c0b346b9c4e81de83162ad","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788213790620,"sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-acfd29e6868d022f","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/types/result.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/types/result.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Result\n2: \n3: `Result<T, E>`: a `T` on success or an `E` on failure. The prelude declares it as an\n4: ordinary [enum](enum.md) with two type parameters -- `enum Result<T, E> { ok(T), err(E) }`\n5: -- so it carries no machinery beyond what any generic enum gets. Unlike [`Opt`](opt.md),\n6: `Result` is not special-cased: both variants are payload variants, and serialization keeps\n7: the single-key wrapper rather than collapsing one side to `null`.\n8: \n9: ```toylang\n10: fn safe_div(pair: {a: Int, b: Int}) -> Result<Int, Str> =\n11:     ok(pair.a / pair.b) if pair.b != 0 else err(\"division by zero\")\n12: \n13: {good: safe_div({a: 10, b: 2}), bad: safe_div({a: 10, b: 0})}\n14: ```\n15: \n16: ```output\n17: {\"good\":{\"ok\":5},\"bad\":{\"err\":\"division by zero\"}}\n18: ```\n19: \n20: Consumption is the same [match](../operators/match.md) any enum uses, closed over `ok` and\n21: `err`:\n22: \n23: ```case\n24: result_match_both_arms\n25: ```\n26: \n27: There is no builtin that produces a `Result` and no `!` for it -- `!` stays Opt's, peeling\n28: tagged absence, which is a different question from which of two payloads a value holds.\n29: A program builds and reads `Result` values entirely by hand, the same as any other\n30: two-variant generic enum, until a decision gives specific operations (`safe_div`, an\n31: unwrap-for-Result, or similar) their own producers.\n\n(End of file - total 31 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Result\n\n`Result<T, E>`: a `T` on success or an `E` on failure. The prelude declares it as an\nordinary [enum](enum.md) with two type parameters -- `enum Result<T, E> { ok(T), err(E) }`\n-- so it carries no machinery beyond what any generic enum gets. Unlike [`Opt`](opt.md),\n`Result` is not special-cased: both variants are payload variants, and serialization keeps\nthe single-key wrapper rather than collapsing one side to `null`.\n\n```toylang\nfn safe_div(pair: {a: Int, b: Int}) -> Result<Int, Str> =\n    ok(pair.a / pair.b) if pair.b != 0 else err(\"division by zero\")\n\n{good: safe_div({a: 10, b: 2}), bad: safe_div({a: 10, b: 0})}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"good\":{\"ok\":5},\"bad\":{\"err\":\"division by zero\"}}\n```\n\nConsumption is the same [match](../operators/match.md) any enum uses, closed over `ok` and","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/types/result.md","text":"# Result\n\n`Result<T, E>`: a `T` on success or an `E` on failure. The prelude declares it as an\nordinary [enum](enum.md) with two type parameters -- `enum Result<T, E> { ok(T), err(E) }`\n-- so it carries no machinery beyond what any generic enum gets. Unlike [`Opt`](opt.md),\n`Result` is not special-cased: both variants are payload variants, and serialization keeps\nthe single-key wrapper rather than collapsing one side to `null`.\n\n```toylang\nfn safe_div(pair: {a: Int, b: Int}) -> Result<Int, Str> =\n    ok(pair.a / pair.b) if pair.b != 0 else err(\"division by zero\")\n\n{good: safe_div({a: 10, b: 2}), bad: safe_div({a: 10, b: 0})}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"good\":{\"ok\":5},\"bad\":{\"err\":\"division by zero\"}}\n```\n\nConsumption is the same [match](../operators/match.md) any enum uses, closed over `ok` and\n`err`:\n\n```case\nresult_match_both_arms\n```\n\nThere is no builtin that produces a `Result` and no `!` for it -- `!` stays Opt's, peeling\ntagged absence, which is a different question from which of two payloads a value holds.\nA program builds and reads `Result` values entirely by hand, the same as any other\ntwo-variant generic enum, until a decision gives specific operations (`safe_div`, an\nunwrap-for-Result, or similar) their own producers.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":31,"totalLines":31,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/reference/types/result.md","time":{"start":1788213790606,"end":1788213790617}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_059d90691001s2gZ50DiEexTaA","sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","messageID":"msg_059d90376001645cGPO2vybcWS"}}
