{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788213795674,"sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a52e965dd56e7990","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/types/vec.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/types/vec.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Vec\n2: \n3: `Vec<T>`: one dimension of entries, all of type `T`, addressed by position. The glossary\n4: ([CONTEXT.md](../../../CONTEXT.md)) calls the axis a dimension and its entry count the\n5: extent; a `Vec<Vec<Int>>` has two dimensions, fixed by the type in order.\n6: \n7: A `Vec` literal builds one from its entries:\n8: \n9: ```toylang\n10: [1, 2, 3]\n11: ```\n12: \n13: ```output\n14: [1,2,3]\n15: ```\n16: \n17: The entries are where the element type comes from -- unless the position already says what\n18: the `Vec` holds. A declared type flows into the expression it annotates, so under a return\n19: annotation an empty `[]` takes its element type from the signature:\n20: \n21: ```toylang\n22: fn nothing() -> Vec<Int> = []\n23: \n24: nothing()\n25: ```\n26: \n27: ```output\n28: []\n29: ```\n30: \n31: Where nothing expects anything, an empty literal still names no element type and is refused:\n32: \n33: ```toylang\n34: []\n35: ```\n36: \n37: ```error\n38: cannot tell what `[]` contains (at byte 0)\n39: ```\n40: \n41: What applies to a `Vec`: the index specs (`[0]`, `[-1]`, `[]` mid-chain -- see\n42: [specs](../operators/specs.md)), [`length`](../builtins/length.md),\n43: [`flatten`](../builtins/flatten.md), [`tail`](../builtins/tail.md),\n44: [`sort`](../builtins/sort.md), [`reverse`](../builtins/reverse.md), the subject-fed\n45: [`select`](../builtins/select.md) and [`map`](../builtins/map.md), and `+`, which joins two\n46: `Vec`s of the same element type (see [arithmetic](../operators/arithmetic.md)).\n47: \n48: Entries of a dimension need not have equal extents one dimension down; the glossary calls\n49: such a value ragged, and collapsing an inner dimension of one yields `Opt` where entries are\n50: missing rather than failing:\n51: \n52: ```case\n53: index_ragged_inner\n54: ```\n\n(End of file - total 54 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Vec\n\n`Vec<T>`: one dimension of entries, all of type `T`, addressed by position. The glossary\n([CONTEXT.md](../../../CONTEXT.md)) calls the axis a dimension and its entry count the\nextent; a `Vec<Vec<Int>>` has two dimensions, fixed by the type in order.\n\nA `Vec` literal builds one from its entries:\n\n```toylang\n[1, 2, 3]\n```\n\n```output\n[1,2,3]\n```\n\nThe entries are where the element type comes from -- unless the position already says what\nthe `Vec` holds. A declared type flows into the expression it annotates, so under a return\nannotation an empty `[]` takes its element type from the signature:\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/types/vec.md","text":"# Vec\n\n`Vec<T>`: one dimension of entries, all of type `T`, addressed by position. The glossary\n([CONTEXT.md](../../../CONTEXT.md)) calls the axis a dimension and its entry count the\nextent; a `Vec<Vec<Int>>` has two dimensions, fixed by the type in order.\n\nA `Vec` literal builds one from its entries:\n\n```toylang\n[1, 2, 3]\n```\n\n```output\n[1,2,3]\n```\n\nThe entries are where the element type comes from -- unless the position already says what\nthe `Vec` holds. A declared type flows into the expression it annotates, so under a return\nannotation an empty `[]` takes its element type from the signature:\n\n```toylang\nfn nothing() -> Vec<Int> = []\n\nnothing()\n```\n\n```output\n[]\n```\n\nWhere nothing expects anything, an empty literal still names no element type and is refused:\n\n```toylang\n[]\n```\n\n```error\ncannot tell what `[]` contains (at byte 0)\n```\n\nWhat applies to a `Vec`: the index specs (`[0]`, `[-1]`, `[]` mid-chain -- see\n[specs](../operators/specs.md)), [`length`](../builtins/length.md),\n[`flatten`](../builtins/flatten.md), [`tail`](../builtins/tail.md),\n[`sort`](../builtins/sort.md), [`reverse`](../builtins/reverse.md), the subject-fed\n[`select`](../builtins/select.md) and [`map`](../builtins/map.md), and `+`, which joins two\n`Vec`s of the same element type (see [arithmetic](../operators/arithmetic.md)).\n\nEntries of a dimension need not have equal extents one dimension down; the glossary calls\nsuch a value ragged, and collapsing an inner dimension of one yields `Opt` where entries are\nmissing rather than failing:\n\n```case\nindex_ragged_inner\n```","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":54,"totalLines":54,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/reference/types/vec.md","time":{"start":1788213795670,"end":1788213795673}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_059d91a6f001SXIYYGjodaOiZK","sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","messageID":"msg_059d916a9001gk4u22k22D52ha"}}
