{"type":"text","timestamp":1788213898033,"sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","part":{"id":"prt_059da3ddf001uH5GpVgfoH61Kv","messageID":"msg_059d95944001Fupe2e2CbpL30I","sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","type":"text","text":"This is a large mechanical change across many docs files. Let me write a Python script to add terminators to all fn/type declarations in docs toylang fences, then validate with the docs test.\n\n","time":{"start":1788213870047,"end":1788213898031}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788213898037,"sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-87eed77a8cb2b6f6","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/add_terminators.py","content":"import os\nimport re\n\nROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))\n\nDECL_START = re.compile(r'^(fn|type|pub fn)\\b')\n\n\ndef add_semicolon(line: str) -> str:\n    body = line.rstrip('\\n')\n    stripped = body.rstrip()\n    if stripped.endswith(';'):\n        return line\n    return body[: len(body) - len(body.lstrip())] + stripped + ';' + '\\n'\n\n\ndef transform(text: str) -> str:\n    lines = text.splitlines(keepends=True)\n    decl_open = False\n    body_last = -1\n    for i, line in enumerate(lines):\n        s = line.rstrip('\\n')\n        if DECL_START.match(s):\n            if decl_open and body_last >= 0:\n                lines[body_last] = add_semicolon(lines[body_last])\n            decl_open = True\n            body_last = i\n            continue\n        if not decl_open:\n            continue\n        if s.strip() == '':\n            lines[body_last] = add_semicolon(lines[body_last])\n            decl_open = False\n            body_last = -1\n        elif s[:1] in (' ', '\\t'):\n            body_last = i\n        else:\n            lines[body_last] = add_semicolon(lines[body_last])\n            decl_open = False\n            body_last = -1\n    if decl_open and body_last >= 0:\n        lines[body_last] = add_semicolon(lines[body_last])\n    return ''.join(lines)\n\n\ndef main():\n    changed = []\n    for dirpath, _, files in os.walk(os.path.join(ROOT, 'docs')):\n        for f in files:\n            if not f.endswith('.md'):\n                continue\n            path = os.path.join(dirpath, f)\n            with open(path) as fh:\n                text = fh.read()\n            new = transform(text)\n            if new != text:\n                with open(path, 'w') as fh:\n                    fh.write(new)\n                changed.append(path)\n    for path in changed:\n        print(path)\n    print(f'{len(changed)} files changed')\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/add_terminators.py","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"add_terminators.py","time":{"start":1788213898028,"end":1788213898035}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"This is a very large set of files. The `docs/examples/euler/*.md` files contain many fn declarations. These are example programs pinned in docs. The docs harness runs them.\n\nThis is a big mechanical change. There are also `examples/*.toy` files and `prelude.toy` (already modified by prior runs). And the euler docs.\n\nThis is a huge amount of manual editing. Given the scale (dozens of files, ~100+ fn declarations), doing this by hand with edit tool would be extremely tedious and error-prone. \n\nThe prior runs modified the corpus .yaml files (107 files) but apparently did NOT touch the docs/examples/euler/*.md, docs/tutorial, docs/guides, docs/reference/builtins, docs/overview. These all still need `;`.\n\nWait — but the brief says the prior run got to `just check` with all corpus/snapshot failures being byte offsets. Did they run the docs tests? The docs.rs harness runs all these fragments. If these docs fragments lack `;`, `just check` (which runs `cargo nextest run` on all tests) would fail on docs.rs too.\n\nHmm, but the brief said \"every remaining tests/corpus/*.yaml + tests/snapshots/*.snap failure is just a byte-offset shift\". Maybe the docs fragments were NOT failing because... let me reconsider. Before the `;` change, the docs fragments parsed fine (fn without `;` was legal). Now they don't. So docs.rs will fail.\n\nGiven the scale, I should write a Python script to do this transformation: for every fn/type declaration in docs, add `;` at the end of the declaration body. But that's tricky because the body can span multiple lines (e.g., `fn safe_div(...) =\\n    ok(...)`).\n\nActually, a simpler and more robust approach: the terminator is required after the fn body expression. But identifying where a fn body ends in arbitrary docs is hard programmatically.\n\nAlternative: Let me reconsider whether these docs fragments actually need updating for `just check` to pass. Let me actually just run the docs test to see what fails. But `just check` runs everything. Let me run the docs test specifically to gauge the scope.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole approach. The cleanest path that handles ALL of this reliably: run the full `just check` (or `cargo test`) and fix failures as they come. But the scale is large.\n\nLet me think about the fn body boundaries. A fn declaration:\n- `fn name(params) -> Ret = body;` — body is the rest until `;`.\n- For single-line bodies like `fn double(x: Int) -> Int = x * 2`, adding `;` at line end is trivial.\n- For multi-line bodies like:\n```\nfn safe_div(...) -> Result<Int, Str> =\n    ok(pair.a / pair.b) if pair.b != 0 else err(\"division by zero\")\n```\nthe `;` goes after the last line of the body.\n\nFor the euler examples, many are single-line. Let me look at how many are single vs multi-line.\n\nThis is genuinely large. Given AGENTS.md's guidance about not manufacturing things and doing real work, I think the right move is to handle this with a Python script that I write, which adds `;` to fn/type declarations. But the complexity of multi-line detection...\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe there's a simpler observation: perhaps these docs examples with `;` are handled differently. Let me check whether the docs.rs harness actually runs these euler example fragments, or whether they're excluded. Let me look at docs.rs more carefully — maybe euler examples have a special fence type.\n\nActually, let me check: the euler examples in docs — do they have `toylang` fences with output fences? Let me look at one.\n\nActually, let me step back and just run the relevant test to see the actual failures and their count. Let me run `cargo test --test docs` (or however it's named) to see. But that compiles too. Since the build is cached now, it should be quick-ish.\n\nLet me run just the docs test.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the scale issue more cleverly. All these fn bodies — I can write a Python script that, for each .md and .toy file, adds a `;` terminator to fn and type declarations. The challenge is multi-line bodies. \n\nBut here's a key insight: the body is one expression. For the script, I can add `;` after each declaration by finding `fn ... =` and then the declaration ends at a line that is NOT a continuation. Continuation lines in this syntax start with whitespace (indented continuation like `    triangle(...)`) OR end with a binary operator / pipe. Actually from emit_toylang, wrapped bodies have continuation lines indented by 4 spaces. And expressions like `a +\\n    b` — the continuation is indented.\n\nActually, looking at the corpus programs, the style is: a wrapped fn body is indented with 4 spaces on continuation lines. The last line of the declaration (the body) is at 4-space indent too. So how do we know where the body ends and the next declaration begins?\n\nFor single-line bodies, it's trivial. For multi-line, the body lines are indented. The next `fn`/`type`/expression would be at column 0. So: a declaration starts at a line matching `^(fn |type |pub fn )`. The body spans subsequent lines that are indented (start with whitespace) until a line at column 0 (start of next declaration or program expression).\n\nSo the algorithm for each file:\n- Find declaration start lines: `^fn `, `^type `, `^pub fn `.\n- After the `=` on the declaration line, if the body fits on that line, add `;` at end of that line.\n- If not (body continues on following indented lines), add `;` to the end of the last indented line before the next column-0 line.\n\nBut this could misfire if a body line ends without being \"indented\"... Actually all continuation lines of a wrapped body ARE indented (emit pads with INDENT=4). And the closing of a record literal `}` on its own line might be at column 0? Unlikely in these examples. Let me check.\n\nHmm, this is getting risky. Given AGENTS.md's emphasis on correctness and not breaking things, and given the scale, let me consider delegating this to a subagent? No — I should do the work.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me look at whether there are many multi-line fn declarations in the docs. Let me count. From the grep, multi-line fn declarations (those ending with `=` and continuing) include: 06-matching (several), 05-streams, 03-pipes, 02-records, guides, euler many.\n\nThis is definitely a large task. Let me write a Python script to add terminators to fn/type declarations across docs .md and .toy files, then verify with the docs test.\n\nLet me design the script carefully:\n- Process each file.\n- Split into lines.\n- Track: we're inside a declaration body when we've seen `fn`/`type` declaration start and the `=` for the body hasn't been \"completed\".\n- Simpler: For each line, if it starts a declaration (`^fn `, `^type `, `^pub fn `), record that a declaration is open.\n- Add `;` to the last line of the declaration. The declaration ends at the last consecutive non-empty line before the next declaration-start or the program expression.\n\nActually, the cleanest: the terminator goes right after the body expression. Given the formatting convention (continuation lines indented), the declaration body ends at the last line that is either (a) the single declaration line if the body fits, or (b) the last indented continuation line.\n\nLet me just do per-declaration: when I hit a `fn`/`type` start line, I note its index. The declaration body ends at the next line that starts a new top-level construct: `^fn `, `^type `, `^pub fn `, OR a line at column 0 that is NOT part of the body (the program expression). But the program expression is at column 0 too.\n\nDistinguishing body continuation from the program expression: continuation lines are indented (leading spaces). The program expression starts at column 0 (or 4 spaces for the `\\x20` in the maintainer sample... no that was test). In docs, program expressions start at column 0.\n\nSo rule: declaration start = line matching `^(fn|type|pub fn) `. The body continues through lines that are indented (start with space) and are not themselves declaration starts. The body's last line is the last such line before a column-0 non-declaration line or EOF or next declaration.\n\nBut there's risk: a fn body that's a single line at column 0, then blank line, then program expression at column 0. The single-line body is column 0 (not indented). After adding `;`, fine.\n\nConsider:\n```\nfn area(r: {w: Int, h: Int}) -> Int = r.w * r.h\n\narea({w: 3, h: 4})\n```\nDeclaration line at col 0, body fits. Next line is blank, then expression at col 0. So body last line = the fn line. Add `;`.\n\nConsider multi-line:\n```\nfn safe_div(pair: {a: Int, b: Int}) -> Result<Int, Str> =\n    ok(pair.a / pair.b) if pair.b != 0 else err(\"division by zero\")\n\n{good: ...}\n```\nDeclaration line 1 (ends with `=`), continuation line 2 (indented 4), then blank, then col-0 expression. Body last line = line 2. Add `;` to line 2.\n\nBut what if a wrapped body's final line is at column 0? From emit, no — emit pads body continuation with INDENT. But the closing of the whole... no.\n\nWhat about a body that is a record spanning lines like:\n```\nfn f(x: Int) -> {a: Int} =\n    {a: x}\n```\nContinuation indented. Fine.\n\nEdge risk: a fn body whose expression genuinely has a col-0 continuation (not in canonical style). Since these docs are pinned as canonical (the fmt harness), they should follow the convention. So indented continuations.\n\nLet me write the script. For each file, for each line:\n- strip trailing whitespace\n- if line matches `^(fn |type |pub fn )`: it's a declaration start. Record `decl_open = True`, and note the line.\n- We need to add `;` to the last line of the body.\n\nActually, let me do a forward approach: iterate lines; maintain `body_last_idx` and a flag. When we encounter a declaration start line at index i:\n  - Close the previous declaration by adding `;` to line body_last_idx (if any declaration is open).\n  - Set current declaration open, body_last_idx = i.\n- For any subsequent line, if it is indented (starts with space) OR is empty, and a declaration is open:\n  - if non-empty, update body_last_idx to this line (continuation).\n  - Actually if empty line, body may continue after blank? No — blank lines separate declarations. But wrapped bodies don't have blank lines inside. So an empty line ends the body.\n  - if line is a column-0 non-declaration line (program expression), the body ended before it.\n- Simpler two-pass: identify all declaration start indices. For each declaration starting at index i, the body's last line is:\n  - If line i itself contains `=` and the rest is non-empty and is the whole body (i.e., next line is blank or a new declaration or a col-0 expression), then body last = i.\n  - Else body continues on i+1, i+2, ... until the line before the next blank OR next declaration OR next col-0 expression. body last = that index.\n\nLet me implement: for declaration at index i, scan forward j from i+1. The body continues as long as line j is non-empty AND (indented OR ...). Stop at first empty line, first declaration-start line, or first column-0 non-empty line that doesn't look like a continuation. Body last = j-1.\n\nThen add `;` to body last line (only if it doesn't already end with `;`).\n\nAlso need to handle `type X = ...;` — same logic. `type` with record body could be multi-line too. e.g. prelude has `type`? Let me not worry, same algorithm.\n\nAlso handle `pub fn`.\n\nLet me also handle the `.toy` example files and prelude.toy — but those were already modified by prior runs? Let me check. The git status shows examples/*.toy and prelude.toy as modified. So the prior run already added `;` to those. Good. But are there other .toy files? Let me check examples dir.\n\nActually the prior run modified examples/adults.toy, greet_fn.toy, shapes.toy, prelude.toy. Are there more examples/*.toy that DON'T have fn/type? Let me not touch those.\n\nBut docs/examples/euler/*.md — these are docs fences, NOT modified by prior runs. They need updating.\n\nLet me write the script to handle only .md files under docs/ (all toylang fences) and check. Actually the script should only touch lines inside toylang fences, not prose. But prose lines won't match `^fn ` / `^type ` patterns, and won't be affected. However, adding `;` only happens to fn/type declarations which only appear in fences. But a body continuation detection across prose... Since I only modify lines that are fn/type declaration lines or their indented continuations, and prose doesn't have `^fn `/`^type ` at column 0 with indented continuations followed by expressions... risk is low. But to be safe, I'll only modify lines that are part of a declaration (start line + its indented continuation lines). Prose is safe because it won't be a declaration start and won't be identified as a continuation (unless prose is indented and follows a fn line, which won't happen).\n\nHmm, but the `case` and `output`/`error` fences are not toylang. The fn declarations only exist in toylang fences. Let me just process all lines but only add `;` to declaration-related lines.\n\nLet me write the script.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether modifying via script and then having the fmt check / docs test validate is enough. The docs test will catch any misfires (e.g., a `;` added in the wrong place → compile error → docs test fails). And the fmt test will catch formatting issues. So I can iterate: run script, run docs test + fmt test, fix.\n\nLet me also reconsider: the error fences in docs have byte offsets that will shift. E.g. int64.md error at byte 27 → now +1 = 28. enum.md error at byte 73 → +1 = 74 (one `;` added before it). stream.md at byte 0 (unchanged). vec.md at byte 0 (unchanged). 06-matching, guides, etc. may have error fences too. The script won't fix those offsets; I'll need to update them after running the docs test which prints actual messages.\n\nOK let me write the script. I'll write it to /tmp? No — /tmp writes are denied. I'll write it in the worktree root (scratch file, won't git add it). The brief says create files with write tool. Let me write `add_terminators.py` in worktree root.\n\nLet me write the script to operate on all .md files under docs/ and all .toy files under examples/ and prelude.toy (in case, but those are already done — running again should be idempotent since they already have `;`... but my script must not double-add; it checks for existing `;`).\n\nWait, the .toy files already have `;` (from prior run). My script should skip lines already ending in `;`. Good, idempotent.\n\nLet me write the script.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the body-continuation detection once more, because it's the risk. Let me define:\n\nFor each line index i, strip trailing whitespace → `s = line.rstrip()`.\nA line \"starts a declaration\" if `re.match(r'^(fn|type|pub fn)\\b', s)`.\nA line is \"indented\" if `s.startswith((' ', '\\t'))` and non-empty.\n\nAlgorithm:\n```\ndeclaration_start = None  # index of current open declaration\nbody_last = None\n\nfor i, line in enumerate(lines):\n    s = line.rstrip('\\n').rstrip()  # actually preserve newline\n    stripped = s.strip()\n    is_decl_start = re.match(r'^(fn|type|pub fn)\\b', s) is not None\n    if is_decl_start:\n        # close previous declaration\n        if declaration_start is not None and body_last is not None:\n            lines[body_last] = add_semicolon(lines[body_last])\n        declaration_start = i\n        body_last = i\n        continue\n    if declaration_start is not None:\n        if stripped == '':\n            # blank line ends the body\n            close and reset\n            lines[body_last] = add_semicolon(lines[body_last])\n            declaration_start = None; body_last = None\n        elif s[0] in ' \\t':\n            # indented continuation -> extends body\n            body_last = i\n        else:\n            # column-0 non-declaration line -> program expression, body ended\n            close and reset\n            lines[body_last] = add_semicolon(...)\n            declaration_start = None; body_last = None\n# at EOF close if open\n```\n\nBut wait — a single-line fn body at column 0: declaration_start=i, body_last=i. Next line is blank → close, add `;` to line i. Good.\n\nA single-line fn followed directly by another fn (no blank): next line is decl_start → close previous (add `;` to previous body line = its own line i). Good.\n\nA multi-line body:\n```\nfn safe_div(...) =\n    ok(...)\n\n{good: ...}\n```\ndeclaration_start=0, body_last=0. Line1: stripped `ok(...)` non-empty, indented → body_last=1. Line2: blank → close, add `;` to line1. Good.\n\nBut there's a subtle case: what if a wrapped body continuation line is at column 0? E.g. emit produces:\n```\nfn f(x) -> Int =\n    x\n```\nContinuation indented, fine. But what about the program expression being indented? In docs the program expression is at column 0. Good.\n\nAnother subtle case: a declaration whose body fits on the line, but the line is LONG and wraps? No, wrapping is manual.\n\nAnother case: `fn area(r: {w: Int, h: Int}) -> Int = r.w * r.h` followed by blank then expression. Good.\n\nEdge: a fn declaration where the body itself contains a multiline record with the closing brace at column 0? Unlikely in canonical format.\n\nNow the maintainer sample in tests/fmt.rs I already edited manually. Good.\n\nWhat about the prelude.toy? Let me check if prelude has `type` declarations. The prior run modified it. My script processes it but it already has `;`, so skip.\n\nActually, wait — I should double check: does prelude.toy or the examples have any issue? They're already handled. Let me run the script only on docs/ .md files to be safe, plus not touch .toy (already done).\n\nLet me also handle the special `\\x20` (4-space) indented continuation — that's just spaces, fine.\n\nNow, also there's a concern: some docs fragments are intentionally NON-parsing and exempt, e.g., functions.md uses `# fmt: syntax-example`. The docs.rs harness runs them regardless and requires output/refuses/error fences. functions.md examples have output fences, so they must compile → need `;`. I already added `;` to functions.md and programs.md manually.\n\nBut wait, functions.md fragment line 9 `fn double(x: Int) -> Int = x * 2;` — my script would also process it but it already has `;`. Good.\n\nLet me now also double-check: are there docs fragments that are deliberately broken and exempt from the harness? The docs.rs harness doesn't have an exemption for non-parsing (per its comment \"There is no way for a fragment to opt out\"). So every toylang fence must compile. So ALL fn/type declarations in ALL docs toylang fences need `;`. My script should cover all .md files under docs/.\n\nLet me write and run the script. Then run docs test.\n\nLet me write the script now.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_059da3f47001vfavF70Lzem76S","sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","messageID":"msg_059d95944001Fupe2e2CbpL30I"}}
