{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788213904614,"sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8866e3cfe6e542c8","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/tutorial/06-matching.md","limit":45,"offset":6},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/tutorial/06-matching.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n6: \n7: ```toylang\n8: fn grade(score: Int) -> Str =\n9:         score | . >= 90 -> \"A\" or . >= 80 -> \"B\" or . >= 70 -> \"C\" or \"F\";\n10: \n11: {a: grade(95), b: grade(82), c: grade(55)}\n12: ```\n13: \n14: ```output\n15: {\"a\":\"A\",\"b\":\"B\",\"c\":\"F\"}\n16: ```\n17: \n18: `.` stays the subject through a guard arm, in both the test and the body -- unlike a bare\n19: payload arm, which rebinds it. The trailing `\"F\"` is a bare expression, the chain's default,\n20: and only the last element can be one. Nothing checks that: an `or` following something that\n21: is not a finished arm is read as Bool disjunction, so a bare expression in the middle becomes\n22: a disjunct of whatever follows it, and is refused for not being a `Bool`:\n23: \n24: ```toylang\n25: 1 | . == 1 -> \"one\" or \"other\" or . == 2 -> \"two\"\n26: ```\n27: \n28: ```error\n29: expected Bool, found Str (at byte 23)\n30: ```\n31: \n32: ## `or`, twice\n33: \n34: That is the same `or` doing both jobs. A guard is still being read when its `or` arrives, so\n35: the `or` joins clauses into one test; a body is finished, so the `or` after it ends the arm:\n36: \n37: ```toylang\n38: fn size(n: Int) -> Str =\n39:         n | . == 0 or . == 1 -> \"tiny\" or . < 10 -> \"small\" or \"big\";\n40: \n41: {a: size(0), b: size(4), c: size(40)}\n42: ```\n43: \n44: ```output\n45: {\"a\":\"tiny\",\"b\":\"small\",\"c\":\"big\"}\n46: ```\n47: \n48: The one thing this costs: a Bool `or` written directly in an arm's body needs parens, since\n49: the bare spelling there is the separator. `and` and `not` have no such wrinkle --\n50: [the boolean operators reference](../reference/operators/boolean.md) covers all three.\n\n(Showing lines 6-50 of 142. Use offset=51 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"\n```toylang\nfn grade(score: Int) -> Str =\n        score | . >= 90 -> \"A\" or . >= 80 -> \"B\" or . >= 70 -> \"C\" or \"F\";\n\n{a: grade(95), b: grade(82), c: grade(55)}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"a\":\"A\",\"b\":\"B\",\"c\":\"F\"}\n```\n\n`.` stays the subject through a guard arm, in both the test and the body -- unlike a bare\npayload arm, which rebinds it. The trailing `\"F\"` is a bare expression, the chain's default,\nand only the last element can be one. Nothing checks that: an `or` following something that\nis not a finished arm is read as Bool disjunction, so a bare expression in the middle becomes\na disjunct of whatever follows it, and is refused for not being a `Bool`:\n\n```toylang\n1 | . == 1 -> \"one\" or \"other\" or . == 2 -> \"two\"","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/tutorial/06-matching.md","text":"\n```toylang\nfn grade(score: Int) -> Str =\n        score | . >= 90 -> \"A\" or . >= 80 -> \"B\" or . >= 70 -> \"C\" or \"F\";\n\n{a: grade(95), b: grade(82), c: grade(55)}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"a\":\"A\",\"b\":\"B\",\"c\":\"F\"}\n```\n\n`.` stays the subject through a guard arm, in both the test and the body -- unlike a bare\npayload arm, which rebinds it. The trailing `\"F\"` is a bare expression, the chain's default,\nand only the last element can be one. Nothing checks that: an `or` following something that\nis not a finished arm is read as Bool disjunction, so a bare expression in the middle becomes\na disjunct of whatever follows it, and is refused for not being a `Bool`:\n\n```toylang\n1 | . == 1 -> \"one\" or \"other\" or . == 2 -> \"two\"\n```\n\n```error\nexpected Bool, found Str (at byte 23)\n```\n\n## `or`, twice\n\nThat is the same `or` doing both jobs. A guard is still being read when its `or` arrives, so\nthe `or` joins clauses into one test; a body is finished, so the `or` after it ends the arm:\n\n```toylang\nfn size(n: Int) -> Str =\n        n | . == 0 or . == 1 -> \"tiny\" or . < 10 -> \"small\" or \"big\";\n\n{a: size(0), b: size(4), c: size(40)}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"a\":\"tiny\",\"b\":\"small\",\"c\":\"big\"}\n```\n\nThe one thing this costs: a Bool `or` written directly in an arm's body needs parens, since\nthe bare spelling there is the separator. `and` and `not` have no such wrinkle --\n[the boolean operators reference](../reference/operators/boolean.md) covers all three.","lineStart":6,"lineEnd":50,"totalLines":142,"truncated":true}},"title":"docs/tutorial/06-matching.md","time":{"start":1788213904598,"end":1788213904612}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me spot-check a few transformed files to make sure the script didn't misbehave, particularly multi-line fn declarations and error-fence byte offsets. Let me check docs/tutorial/06-matching.md and docs/examples/euler/29-distinct-powers.md (many multi-line fns).","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_059dac2fb001ZB8vZ0OASYnQt8","sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","messageID":"msg_059dab52b0016BBpoNYIAw287J"}}
