{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788213917344,"sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-ac6cce837b76ac02","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/tutorial/06-matching.md","limit":60,"offset":95},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/tutorial/06-matching.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n95: \n96: ```toylang\n97: fn tags(n: Int) -> Opt<Vec<Int>> = n | . > 0 -> [];\n98: \n99: {a: tags(1), b: tags(-1)}\n100: ```\n101: \n102: ```output\n103: {\"a\":[],\"b\":null}\n104: ```\n105: \n106: When a partial chain's arms are themselves `Opt`-typed, the wrapping doubles: \"no arm\n107: matched\" and \"matched, and found nothing\" are two different values in memory (`none` and\n108: `some(none)`), even though both print `null` once serialization flattens the tags away.\n109: The return type says so honestly:\n110: \n111: ```toylang\n112: fn first_reading(entry: {valid: Bool, readings: Vec<Int>}) -> Opt<Opt<Int>> =\n113:         entry | .valid -> entry.readings[9];\n114: \n115: first_reading({valid: 1 == 2, readings: [5]})\n116: ```\n117: \n118: ```output\n119: null\n120: ```\n121: \n122: A default arm collapses the doubling, because the chain is no longer partial:\n123: \n124: ```toylang\n125: fn first_reading(entry: {valid: Bool, readings: Vec<Int>}) -> Opt<Int> =\n126:         entry | .valid -> entry.readings[0] or entry.readings[9];\n127: \n128: {\n129:     a: first_reading({valid: 1 == 2, readings: [5]}),\n130:     b: first_reading({valid: 1 == 1, readings: [1]})\n131: }\n132: ```\n133: \n134: ```output\n135: {\"a\":null,\"b\":1}\n136: ```\n137: \n138: That is the core of the language. From here: the [matching guide](../guides/matching.md) for\n139: choosing between if/else and match and handling a partial chain's result, the other\n140: [guides](../guides/enums.md) for feature-sized tasks, the reference for every builtin, type,\n141: and operator, and Examples for every corpus program with the code all seven backends compile\n142: it to.\n\n(End of file - total 142 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"\n```toylang\nfn tags(n: Int) -> Opt<Vec<Int>> = n | . > 0 -> [];\n\n{a: tags(1), b: tags(-1)}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"a\":[],\"b\":null}\n```\n\nWhen a partial chain's arms are themselves `Opt`-typed, the wrapping doubles: \"no arm\nmatched\" and \"matched, and found nothing\" are two different values in memory (`none` and\n`some(none)`), even though both print `null` once serialization flattens the tags away.\nThe return type says so honestly:\n\n```toylang\nfn first_reading(entry: {valid: Bool, readings: Vec<Int>}) -> Opt<Opt<Int>> =\n        entry | .valid -> entry.readings[9];\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/tutorial/06-matching.md","text":"\n```toylang\nfn tags(n: Int) -> Opt<Vec<Int>> = n | . > 0 -> [];\n\n{a: tags(1), b: tags(-1)}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"a\":[],\"b\":null}\n```\n\nWhen a partial chain's arms are themselves `Opt`-typed, the wrapping doubles: \"no arm\nmatched\" and \"matched, and found nothing\" are two different values in memory (`none` and\n`some(none)`), even though both print `null` once serialization flattens the tags away.\nThe return type says so honestly:\n\n```toylang\nfn first_reading(entry: {valid: Bool, readings: Vec<Int>}) -> Opt<Opt<Int>> =\n        entry | .valid -> entry.readings[9];\n\nfirst_reading({valid: 1 == 2, readings: [5]})\n```\n\n```output\nnull\n```\n\nA default arm collapses the doubling, because the chain is no longer partial:\n\n```toylang\nfn first_reading(entry: {valid: Bool, readings: Vec<Int>}) -> Opt<Int> =\n        entry | .valid -> entry.readings[0] or entry.readings[9];\n\n{\n    a: first_reading({valid: 1 == 2, readings: [5]}),\n    b: first_reading({valid: 1 == 1, readings: [1]})\n}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"a\":null,\"b\":1}\n```\n\nThat is the core of the language. From here: the [matching guide](../guides/matching.md) for\nchoosing between if/else and match and handling a partial chain's result, the other\n[guides](../guides/enums.md) for feature-sized tasks, the reference for every builtin, type,\nand operator, and Examples for every corpus program with the code all seven backends compile\nit to.","lineStart":95,"lineEnd":142,"totalLines":142,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/tutorial/06-matching.md","time":{"start":1788213917335,"end":1788213917342}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The script handled these correctly. Note the error fence at line 29: `expected Bool, found Str (at byte 23)`. The program is:\n```\n1 | . == 1 -> \"one\" or \"other\" or . == 2 -> \"two\"\n```\nThis has no fn declaration before it, so byte offset unaffected. Good.\n\nLet me check the sections with fn that have error fences after them (byte offsets that shifted). Let me look at lines 51-142 of 06-matching and the rest.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_059daf4db001Rcr3xjXNrVrI8R","sessionID":"ses_fa62a5b39ffevmxUbcy053FMKH","messageID":"msg_059dac53c0012VFPpIrkiIz1vo"}}
